00:00:57.663 --> 00:00:59.583
Introduction and Why It Matters.

14
00:01:00.833 --> 00:01:01.751
A story:

15
00:01:02.031 --> 00:01:05.636
38 customers paid for orders that no longer existed.

16
00:01:05.916 --> 00:01:11.057
At 9:12 pm on a Friday, the disk under the orders database primary failed.

17
00:01:11.337 --> 00:01:19.679
The failover manager did exactly what it was configured to do: it promoted the healthiest asynchronous replica within 25 seconds.

18
00:01:19.959 --> 00:01:23.436
Checkout recovered, and the on-call engineer went back to dinner.

19
00:01:23.716 --> 00:01:26.127
On Monday, support had 38 tickets:

20
00:01:26.407 --> 00:01:29.201
Money debited, but the app says I have no order.

21
00:01:29.481 --> 00:01:34.900
Razorpay's dashboard showed 38 captured payments in the four seconds before the failure.

22
00:01:35.180 --> 00:01:37.847
The new primary had no trace of those orders.

23
00:01:38.127 --> 00:01:45.017
They had been committed on the old primary and acknowledged to the customers, but they hadn't reached the replica when the disk died.

24
00:01:46.367 --> 00:01:47.647
Nothing was broken.

25
00:01:47.927 --> 00:01:55.671
Asynchronous replication promises that the replica may be behind, and a failover to it must lose whatever it hadn't received yet.

26
00:01:55.951 --> 00:02:05.167
The platform had an RPO (recovery point objective) of a few seconds without anyone having decided that, and no process for the seconds it lost.

27
00:02:05.447 --> 00:02:07.730
The fixes, all covered in this chapter:

28
00:02:08.010 --> 00:02:18.357
Payments and orders commit only after a quorum of standbys has the WAL (ANY 1 (r1, r2)), so a failover to a caught-up standby loses nothing.

29
00:02:18.637 --> 00:02:22.861
Low-value writes opt out per transaction, so they don't pay that latency.

30
00:02:24.211 --> 00:02:29.949
The failover manager fences the old primary, so it can't accept writes after a new one is promoted.

31
00:02:30.229 --> 00:02:35.349
A reconciliation job compares captured payments with orders after every failover.

32
00:02:36.699 --> 00:02:37.382
Quote:

33
00:02:37.662 --> 00:02:39.326
The thesis of this chapter.

34
00:02:39.606 --> 00:02:42.721
Replication gives you copies, and copies disagree.

35
00:02:43.001 --> 00:02:50.553
Every replication design is a choice about which disagreements you'll tolerate, for how long, and what happens when you switch copies.

36
00:02:50.833 --> 00:02:56.315
Make that choice per data class, measure it, and have a plan for the moment the copies diverge.

