189
00:09:32.717 --> 00:09:35.811
If fewer than k standbys are reachable, commits block.

190
00:09:36.091 --> 00:09:37.563
Fully synchronous to all.

191
00:09:37.843 --> 00:09:39.144
Commit returns when...:

192
00:09:39.424 --> 00:09:40.896
All standbys have it.

193
00:09:41.176 --> 00:09:42.733
RPO on leader loss:

194
00:09:43.013 --> 00:09:43.760
0.

195
00:09:44.040 --> 00:09:46.792
Write latency: + round trip to the slowest.

196
00:09:47.072 --> 00:09:48.352
Availability risk:

197
00:09:48.632 --> 00:09:50.659
Any standby failure blocks writes.

198
00:09:52.009 --> 00:09:57.939
PostgreSQL's synchronouscommit levels (per session or per transaction with SET LOCAL):

199
00:09:59.289 --> 00:10:00.633
Here is a quick table:

200
00:10:00.913 --> 00:10:04.156
Level, and Commit waits until..., and Survives. off.

201
00:10:04.436 --> 00:10:05.716
Commit waits until...:

202
00:10:05.996 --> 00:10:10.668
Nothing (WAL flushed asynchronously, ~3 times walwriterdelay).

203
00:10:10.948 --> 00:10:11.865
Survives:

204
00:10:12.145 --> 00:10:17.500
Nothing; the last ~600 ms can be lost even without failover. local.

205
00:10:17.780 --> 00:10:19.060
Commit waits until...:

206
00:10:19.340 --> 00:10:21.239
WAL flushed on the leader.

207
00:10:21.519 --> 00:10:22.436
Survives:

208
00:10:22.716 --> 00:10:25.575
Leader crash with an intact disk. remotewrite.

209
00:10:25.855 --> 00:10:27.135
Commit waits until...:

210
00:10:27.415 --> 00:10:30.529
Standby received the WAL (in OS memory).

211
00:10:30.809 --> 00:10:31.727
Survives:

212
00:10:32.007 --> 00:10:35.548
Leader loss, if the standby OS stays up. on (default).

213
00:10:35.828 --> 00:10:37.108
Commit waits until...:

214
00:10:37.388 --> 00:10:39.799
Standby flushed the WAL to disk.

215
00:10:40.079 --> 00:10:40.996
Survives:

216
00:10:41.276 --> 00:10:43.004
Leader loss. remoteapply.

217
00:10:43.284 --> 00:10:44.564
Commit waits until...:

218
00:10:44.844 --> 00:10:46.124
Standby applied it.

219
00:10:46.404 --> 00:10:47.321
Survives:

220
00:10:47.601 --> 00:10:51.228
Leader loss, and reads on that standby see the write immediately.

221
00:10:52.578 --> 00:10:59.405
The remote levels only wait when synchronousstandbynames is set, and they apply to whichever standbys satisfy it.

222
00:10:59.685 --> 00:11:09.690
The lab (§5, Experiment 3) runs this on real PostgreSQL 16 with synchronousstandbynames = 'ANY 1 (r1, r2)':

223
00:11:11.040 --> 00:11:17.973
On screen: == both standbys down: insert pay3... after 2 s the client is still waiting:

224
00:11:18.253 --> 00:11:23.459
1 backend(s) in waitevent=SyncRep. pay3 visible to other sessions?

225
00:11:23.739 --> 00:11:31.141
0 (not until the wait ends). == operator cancels the stuck wait (pgcancelbackend): client saw:

226
00:11:31.421 --> 00:11:36.605
WARNING: canceling wait for synchronous replication due to user request. client saw:

227
00:11:36.885 --> 00:11:37.760
DETAIL:

228
00:11:38.040 --> 00:11:45.933
The transaction has already committed locally, but might not. have been replicated to the standby. pay3 visible on primary now?

229
00:11:46.213 --> 00:11:50.373
1 - committed locally, on NO standby: a failover now. would lose it.

230
00:11:53.223 --> 00:11:54.141
Watch out:

231
00:11:54.421 --> 00:11:56.618
Synchronous replication has two traps.

232
00:11:56.898 --> 00:12:01.229
Availability: with too few standbys reachable, commits hang (not fail).

233
00:12:01.509 --> 00:12:10.533
Size the quorum (ANY 1 of 2 plus standbys across AZs) so that one failure doesn't stop writes, and alert on waitevent = 'SyncRep'.

234
00:12:10.813 --> 00:12:18.471
Cancelled doesn't mean not committed: a client timeout or cancel during the sync wait leaves the transaction committed on the leader.

235
00:12:18.751 --> 00:12:25.407
Treat it as an unknown outcome (Ch 04): retry idempotently or check, never assume it failed.

236
00:12:26.757 --> 00:12:30.341
Replication lag and the consistency guarantees it breaks.

237
00:12:30.621 --> 00:12:34.483
With async followers, a read from a follower can return stale data.

238
00:12:34.763 --> 00:12:38.133
Three session guarantees fix the anomalies users notice:

239
00:12:39.483 --> 00:12:40.827
Here is a quick table:

240
00:12:41.107 --> 00:12:45.267
Guarantee, and Anomaly it prevents, and Grocery example, and Technique.

241
00:12:45.547 --> 00:12:46.614
Read-your-writes.

242
00:12:46.894 --> 00:12:48.217
Anomaly it prevents:

243
00:12:48.497 --> 00:12:49.862
You don't see your own write.

244
00:12:50.142 --> 00:12:51.379
Grocery example:

245
00:12:51.659 --> 00:12:54.795
Customer cancels an order, refreshes, sees Placed.

246
00:12:55.075 --> 00:12:55.907
Technique:

247
00:12:56.187 --> 00:13:00.838
Read from the leader, or from a follower that has replayed past your write's LSN.

248
00:13:01.118 --> 00:13:02.334
Monotonic reads.

249
00:13:02.614 --> 00:13:03.937
Anomaly it prevents:

250
00:13:04.217 --> 00:13:06.201
Time goes backwards across reads.

251
00:13:06.481 --> 00:13:07.718
Grocery example:

252
00:13:07.998 --> 00:13:12.243
Order shows Cancelled, then Placed on the next refresh (a different follower).

253
00:13:12.523 --> 00:13:13.355
Technique:

254
00:13:13.635 --> 00:13:18.969
Carry the highest LSN you've seen (read or written); only use followers at or beyond it.

255
00:13:19.249 --> 00:13:20.571
Consistent prefix.

256
00:13:20.851 --> 00:13:22.174
Anomaly it prevents:

257
00:13:22.454 --> 00:13:24.267
Seeing an effect before its cause.

258
00:13:24.547 --> 00:13:25.785
Grocery example:

259
00:13:26.065 --> 00:13:28.305
Refund issued before Order cancelled.

260
00:13:28.585 --> 00:13:29.417
Technique:

261
00:13:29.697 --> 00:13:34.134
Single-leader order within a partition; causal tokens across partitions.

262
00:13:35.484 --> 00:13:41.223
The lab reproduces both anomalies on real replicas (§5, Experiment 1):

263
00:13:42.573 --> 00:13:51.106
On screen: == customer cancelled; commit LSN token = 0 or 402BEF0. primary:

264
00:13:51.386 --> 00:13:52.751
CANCELLED. r2:

265
00:13:53.031 --> 00:13:57.789
CANCELLED replayed up to 0 or 402BEF0. r1:

266
00:13:58.069 --> 00:14:16.543
PLACED replayed up to 0 or 402BE78 (paused). == read-your-writes check: is r1 = token? f is r2 = token? t. == monotonic reads violation without a token: read r2 then r1 - CANCELLED then PLACED.

267
00:14:19.393 --> 00:14:28.417
L2 builds a replica selector that enforces both guarantees with one token: the maximum LSN the session has written or read.

268
00:14:28.697 --> 00:14:32.111
Ch 05 L4 covered only the write side.

269
00:14:33.461 --> 00:14:35.189
Quorums in leaderless systems.

270
00:14:35.469 --> 00:14:42.893
With N replicas, a write waits for W acknowledgements and a read queries R replicas and takes the newest version:

271
00:14:44.243 --> 00:14:45.224
The formula:

272
00:14:45.504 --> 00:14:51.584
R + W N Rightarrow every read set overlaps every write set in at least one replica.

273
00:14:52.934 --> 00:15:02.513
L1 simulates N = 3 with replicas applying writes 0 to 40 ms apart, reading 5 ms after the write is acknowledged:

274
00:15:03.863 --> 00:15:04.780
On screen:

275
00:15:05.060 --> 00:15:10.073
W=1 R=1 (R+W = N) stale reads:

276
00:15:10.353 --> 00:15:12.401
54.27%.

277
00:15:12.681 --> 00:15:17.801
W=1 R=2 (R+W = N) stale reads:

278
00:15:18.081 --> 00:15:19.916
22.36%.

279
00:15:20.196 --> 00:15:25.188
W=2 R=1 (R+W = N) stale reads:

280
00:15:25.468 --> 00:15:27.388
22.15%.

281
00:15:27.668 --> 00:15:32.127
W=2 R=2 (R+W N) stale reads:

282
00:15:32.407 --> 00:15:34.433
0.00%.

283
00:15:37.283 --> 00:15:38.755
What quorums don't give you:

284
00:15:40.105 --> 00:15:41.449
Here is a quick table:

285
00:15:41.729 --> 00:15:43.649
Gap, and Why, and Mitigation.

286
00:15:43.929 --> 00:15:45.167
Linearizability.

287
00:15:45.447 --> 00:15:46.129
Why:

288
00:15:46.409 --> 00:15:52.041
A read concurrent with a write may see it on one replica and not another; two readers can disagree.

289
00:15:52.321 --> 00:15:53.324
Mitigation:

290
00:15:53.604 --> 00:15:58.809
Read repair on quorum reads; lightweight transactions (Paxos) for compare-and-set.

291
00:15:59.089 --> 00:16:01.863
Safety under sloppy quorums or hinted handoff.

292
00:16:02.143 --> 00:16:02.825
Why:

293
00:16:03.105 --> 00:16:06.881
Writes accepted by stand-in nodes outside the preferred replicas.

294
00:16:07.161 --> 00:16:08.164
Mitigation:

295
00:16:08.444 --> 00:16:12.412
Know whether your store does this; disable where strict overlap matters.

296
00:16:12.692 --> 00:16:14.740
Correct merges of concurrent writes.

297
00:16:15.020 --> 00:16:15.703
Why:

298
00:16:15.983 --> 00:16:18.905
Two writes with no causal order: someone must pick.

299
00:16:19.185 --> 00:16:20.188
Mitigation:

300
00:16:20.468 --> 00:16:26.612
LWW (lossy), version vectors + app merge, CRDTs (§2.9).

301
00:16:26.892 --> 00:16:30.049
Durability of an acknowledged W = 1 write.

302
00:16:30.329 --> 00:16:31.012
Why:

303
00:16:31.292 --> 00:16:33.596
One replica acknowledged, then died.

304
00:16:33.876 --> 00:16:34.879
Mitigation:

305
00:16:35.159 --> 00:16:38.679
W ≥ 2 for data you care about.

306
00:16:40.029 --> 00:16:51.805
Anti-entropy: read repair (fix stale replicas seen during reads), hinted handoff (deliver writes missed while a node was down), and Merkle-tree repair (periodic full comparison).

307
00:16:52.085 --> 00:16:57.077
Cassandra needs regular repair within gcgraceseconds (Ch 06).

308
00:16:58.427 --> 00:16:59.344
Failover:

309
00:16:59.624 --> 00:17:02.099
RPO, RTO and what can go wrong.

310
00:17:02.379 --> 00:17:03.723
Here is a quick table:

311
00:17:04.003 --> 00:17:05.219
Step, and Pitfall.

312
00:17:05.499 --> 00:17:08.485
Detect leader failure (health checks, lease expiry):

313
00:17:08.765 --> 00:17:13.480
Too aggressive, then false failovers during GC pauses or network blips.

314
00:17:13.760 --> 00:17:15.701
Too slow, then long outages.

315
00:17:15.981 --> 00:17:19.651
Choose a new leader (most up-to-date standby; in the sync quorum):

316
00:17:19.931 --> 00:17:24.347
Choosing a lagging async replica = data loss (§1.1).

317
00:17:24.627 --> 00:17:31.219
Promote it and reconfigure clients (DNS, service endpoint, proxy, multi-host JDBC):

318
00:17:31.499 --> 00:17:35.147
Clients cache the old endpoint and keep writing to the old leader.

319
00:17:35.427 --> 00:17:38.797
Fence the old leader (demote, stop, cut network, STONITH):

320
00:17:39.077 --> 00:17:41.765
If the old leader is alive, you have split brain.

321
00:17:42.045 --> 00:17:45.629
Rejoin the old leader as a follower (pgrewind, rebuild):

322
00:17:45.909 --> 00:17:48.661
Writes it accepted after the fork are discarded.

323
00:17:50.011 --> 00:17:51.355
Here is a quick table:

324
00:17:51.635 --> 00:17:56.393
Metric, and Definition, and Async replica failover, and Quorum-sync failover.

325
00:17:56.673 --> 00:17:57.590
RPO.

326
00:17:57.870 --> 00:17:58.830
Definition:

327
00:17:59.110 --> 00:18:00.390
Data you can lose.

328
00:18:00.670 --> 00:18:02.270
Async replica failover:

329
00:18:02.550 --> 00:18:05.238
Lag at failure time (seconds, sometimes more).

330
00:18:05.518 --> 00:18:06.841
Quorum-sync failover:

331
00:18:07.121 --> 00:18:09.275
0 (if promoting a quorum member).

332
00:18:09.555 --> 00:18:10.451
RTO.

333
00:18:10.731 --> 00:18:11.691
Definition:

334
00:18:11.971 --> 00:18:13.806
Time until writes work again.

335
00:18:14.086 --> 00:18:15.686
Async replica failover:

336
00:18:15.966 --> 00:18:22.857
Detection ( about ttl) + promotion + client reconfiguration: typically 20 to 60 s.

337
00:18:23.137 --> 00:18:24.459
Quorum-sync failover:

338
00:18:24.739 --> 00:18:25.443
Same.

339
00:18:26.793 --> 00:18:28.287
Split brain and fencing.

340
00:18:28.567 --> 00:18:33.601
Split brain happens when two nodes both believe they're the leader and both accept writes.

341
00:18:33.881 --> 00:18:42.948
The lab produces one on real PostgreSQL by promoting a standby while the old primary is still alive (§5, Experiment 4):

342
00:18:44.298 --> 00:18:53.322
On screen: == SPLIT BRAIN: pods with the old endpoint debit on the old primary, new pods debit on. r1. old primary:

343
00:18:53.602 --> 00:18:55.671
30000 new primary r1:

344
00:18:55.951 --> 00:18:59.194
5000 r2 (still following the old primary):

345
00:18:59.474 --> 00:19:00.562
30000.

346
00:19:00.842 --> 00:19:25.738
20000 + 45000 = 65000 debited from a 50000 balance. ... timelines: r1 (primary)=2 p streams timeline 2 r2 streams timeline 2 balances: p=5000 r1=5000 r2=5000. the old side's -20000 debit is GONE: pgrewind discarded every write made after the. fork.

347
00:19:28.588 --> 00:19:44.887
Two lessons from running it for real: pgrewind copies the source's configuration files (the rewound node came back with the wrong port until it was fixed), and r2 also had to be rewound, because it had replayed WAL from the old primary after the fork.

348
00:19:45.167 --> 00:19:46.468
Prevention toolkit:

349
00:19:47.818 --> 00:19:49.162
Here is a quick table:

350
00:19:49.442 --> 00:19:51.405
Mechanism, and How, and Notes.

351
00:19:51.685 --> 00:19:56.101
Consensus-backed leader lease (etcd, ZooKeeper, Consul; Patroni, Stolon).

352
00:19:56.381 --> 00:19:57.042
How:

353
00:19:57.322 --> 00:20:01.205
Only the holder of an unexpired lease in the DCS is leader.

354
00:20:01.485 --> 00:20:02.295
Notes:

355
00:20:02.575 --> 00:20:06.351
Leader must renew; standbys promote only after expiry.

356
00:20:06.631 --> 00:20:07.698
Self-fencing.

357
00:20:07.978 --> 00:20:08.639
How:

358
00:20:08.919 --> 00:20:13.229
A leader that can't renew its lease demotes itself before the lease expires.

359
00:20:13.509 --> 00:20:18.863
Notes: ttl ≥ loopwait + 2 times retrytimeout (Patroni).

360
00:20:19.143 --> 00:20:20.125
Watchdog.

361
00:20:20.405 --> 00:20:21.066
How:

362
00:20:21.346 --> 00:20:25.805
A kernel or hardware timer resets the node if the manager process stalls.

363
00:20:26.085 --> 00:20:26.895
Notes:

364
00:20:27.175 --> 00:20:31.677
Covers GC or VM freezes, where self-fencing can't run in time.

365
00:20:31.957 --> 00:20:33.983
STONITH (shoot the other node in the head).

366
00:20:34.263 --> 00:20:34.925
How:

367
00:20:35.205 --> 00:20:39.237
Power off or isolate the old leader via infrastructure APIs.

368
00:20:39.517 --> 00:20:40.327
Notes:

369
00:20:40.607 --> 00:20:42.933
Classic cluster managers (Pacemaker).

370
00:20:44.283 --> 00:20:45.456
Fencing tokens.

371
00:20:45.736 --> 00:20:46.397
How:

372
00:20:46.677 --> 00:20:53.440
Every write carries a monotonically increasing leader epoch, and the storage or consumer rejects older epochs.

373
00:20:53.720 --> 00:20:54.531
Notes:

374
00:20:54.811 --> 00:20:58.544
The strongest guarantee where you control the storage (Ch 20).

375
00:20:58.824 --> 00:21:00.360
Single writer endpoint.

376
00:21:00.640 --> 00:21:01.301
How:

377
00:21:01.581 --> 00:21:06.488
Clients reach the leader only through a proxy or service updated by the manager.

378
00:21:06.768 --> 00:21:07.579
Notes:

379
00:21:07.859 --> 00:21:11.613
Pods caching the old IP are the usual split-brain source.

380
00:21:12.963 --> 00:21:16.569
The L4 simulation quantifies why the watchdog matters.

381
00:21:16.849 --> 00:21:24.145
Over 1,000,000 simulated partitions with 0.5 % of process stalls lasting 5 to 60 s:

382
00:21:25.495 --> 00:21:40.193
On screen: lease only: two writable primaries in 4,033 cases (0.403%), worst overlap 48.3 s. lease + watchdog: two writable primaries in 0 cases.

383
00:21:43.043 --> 00:21:46.265
Multi-leader replication and conflict resolution.

384
00:21:46.545 --> 00:21:51.515
Conflicts happen whenever two leaders accept writes to the same data without coordinating.

385
00:21:51.795 --> 00:21:54.121
Options, from simplest to most correct:

386
00:21:55.471 --> 00:21:56.815
Here is a quick table:

387
00:21:57.095 --> 00:22:00.145
Strategy, and How, and Loses data?, and Use for.

388
00:22:00.425 --> 00:22:01.599
Avoid conflicts.

389
00:22:01.879 --> 00:22:02.540
How:

390
00:22:02.820 --> 00:22:06.937
Route all writes for a key to one home leader (a customer's home region).

391
00:22:07.217 --> 00:22:08.348
Loses data?:

392
00:22:08.628 --> 00:22:09.311
No.

393
00:22:09.591 --> 00:22:10.444
Use for:

394
00:22:10.724 --> 00:22:12.367
The best option when possible.

395
00:22:12.647 --> 00:22:14.993
Last-write-wins (LWW).

396
00:22:15.273 --> 00:22:15.935
How:

397
00:22:16.215 --> 00:22:18.263
Keep the write with the highest timestamp.

398
00:22:18.543 --> 00:22:19.673
Loses data?:

399
00:22:19.953 --> 00:22:24.156
Yes: silently drops concurrent writes, and clock skew picks the wrong winner.

400
00:22:24.436 --> 00:22:25.289
Use for:

401
00:22:25.569 --> 00:22:28.492
Caches, idempotent overwrites, low-value data.

402
00:22:28.772 --> 00:22:30.799
Domain precedence or state machines.

403
00:22:31.079 --> 00:22:31.740
How:

404
00:22:32.020 --> 00:22:37.588
Merge by business rules (DELIVERED beats OUTFORDELIVERY; CANCELLED before packing beats PACKED).

405
00:22:37.868 --> 00:22:38.999
Loses data?:

406
00:22:39.279 --> 00:22:40.964
No, if the rules are total.

407
00:22:41.244 --> 00:22:42.097
Use for:

408
00:22:42.377 --> 00:22:45.364
Order and delivery status from offline devices.

409
00:22:46.714 --> 00:22:48.549
Version vectors + app merge.

410
00:22:48.829 --> 00:22:49.490
How:

411
00:22:49.770 --> 00:22:53.055
Detect concurrency; present siblings to the app to merge.

412
00:22:53.335 --> 00:22:54.466
Loses data?:

413
00:22:54.746 --> 00:22:55.429
No.

414
00:22:55.709 --> 00:22:56.562
Use for:

415
00:22:56.842 --> 00:22:58.186
Complex documents.

416
00:22:58.466 --> 00:22:59.511
CRDTs.

417
00:22:59.791 --> 00:23:00.453
How:

418
00:23:00.733 --> 00:23:04.957
Data types whose merge is commutative, associative and idempotent:

419
00:23:05.237 --> 00:23:10.975
G-Counter, PN-Counter, OR-Set, LWW-Register, RGA or sequences.

420
00:23:11.255 --> 00:23:12.386
Loses data?:

421
00:23:12.666 --> 00:23:15.482
No (by construction, within the type's semantics).

422
00:23:15.762 --> 00:23:16.615
Use for:

423
00:23:16.895 --> 00:23:19.605
Carts, counters, sets, collaborative text.

424
00:23:20.955 --> 00:23:28.272
L1's real output shows LWW losing an update to a 90 s clock skew, and an OR-Set converging:

425
00:23:29.622 --> 00:23:30.539
On screen:

426
00:23:30.819 --> 00:23:31.502
B.

427
00:23:31.782 --> 00:23:35.345
Last-write-wins with a phone clock 90 s behind the server.

428
00:23:35.625 --> 00:23:47.273
LWW keeps: qty=1 (edited at 20:00:10 real time) ← the newer edit (qty=3) is. silently lost.

429
00:23:47.553 --> 00:23:48.235
C.

430
00:23:48.515 --> 00:24:05.049
OR-Set cart edited offline on two devices. phone sees ATTA-5KG, EGGS-12, web sees MILK-1L. merge(phone, web) = EGGS-12, MILK-1L. merge(web, phone) = EGGS-12, MILK-1L (same: order doesn't matter).

431
00:24:07.899 --> 00:24:15.792
Offline rider app (multi-leader in practice): a rider in a basement marks delivered with the OTP (Ch 01).

432
00:24:16.072 --> 00:24:18.120
The phone is a leader for that action.

433
00:24:18.400 --> 00:24:30.688
On reconnect, the server merges it by domain precedence (a verified delivery OTP is final) and by idempotency keys (Ch 04), not by timestamps from the phone's clock.

434
00:24:32.038 --> 00:24:34.278
Replication inside other systems.

435
00:24:34.558 --> 00:24:35.902
Here is a quick table:

436
00:24:36.182 --> 00:24:38.358
System, and Model, and Key settings.

437
00:24:38.638 --> 00:24:39.875
PostgreSQL.

438
00:24:40.155 --> 00:24:40.902
Model:

439
00:24:41.182 --> 00:24:47.198
Physical streaming (WAL), logical replication (per table, for CDC and upgrades).

440
00:24:47.478 --> 00:24:54.966
Key settings: synchronousstandbynames, synchronouscommit, replication slots (retain WAL), hotstandbyfeedback.

441
00:24:55.246 --> 00:24:56.313
MySQL.

442
00:24:56.593 --> 00:24:57.339
Model:

443
00:24:57.619 --> 00:25:02.249
Binlog replication (async, semi-sync), Group Replication (Paxos).

444
00:25:02.529 --> 00:25:05.110
Key settings: rplsemisync, GTIDs.

445
00:25:05.390 --> 00:25:06.478
MongoDB.

446
00:25:06.758 --> 00:25:07.505
Model:

447
00:25:07.785 --> 00:25:10.003
Replica set with Raft-like elections.

448
00:25:10.283 --> 00:25:15.553
Key settings: w: majority, readConcern: majority/linearizable.

449
00:25:15.833 --> 00:25:16.686
Kafka.

450
00:25:16.966 --> 00:25:17.713
Model:

451
00:25:17.993 --> 00:25:21.043
Leader per partition, in-sync replicas (ISR).

452
00:25:21.323 --> 00:25:30.027
Key settings: acks=all, min.insync.replicas=2, unclean.leader.election.enable=false (Ch 14).

453
00:25:31.377 --> 00:25:32.145
Redis.

454
00:25:32.425 --> 00:25:33.172
Model:

455
00:25:33.452 --> 00:25:37.249
Async leader, then replicas; Sentinel or Cluster failover.

456
00:25:37.529 --> 00:25:38.511
Key settings:

457
00:25:38.791 --> 00:25:43.100
WAIT n ms (reduces, not removes, loss); min-replicas-to-write.

458
00:25:43.380 --> 00:25:44.297
Cassandra.

459
00:25:44.577 --> 00:25:45.324
Model:

460
00:25:45.604 --> 00:25:47.460
Leaderless, RF per DC.

461
00:25:47.740 --> 00:25:48.721
Key settings:

462
00:25:49.001 --> 00:25:51.348
Consistency level per query, repairs.

463
00:25:52.698 --> 00:25:53.338
Tip:

464
00:25:53.618 --> 00:25:56.690
Replication slots: a quiet disk-filling hazard.

465
00:25:56.970 --> 00:26:05.653
A PostgreSQL replication slot retains WAL until its consumer (a standby or a CDC connector) confirms it.

466
00:26:05.933 --> 00:26:10.263
An abandoned slot keeps WAL forever and fills the primary's disk.

467
00:26:10.543 --> 00:26:15.493
Monitor pgreplicationslots (retained bytes) and set maxslotwalkeepsize.

468
00:26:16.843 --> 00:26:18.656
Choosing a replication design.

469
00:26:18.936 --> 00:26:20.280
Here is a quick table:

470
00:26:20.560 --> 00:26:22.075
Question, and Leads to.

471
00:26:22.355 --> 00:26:24.893
Can you lose the last seconds of writes on failover?:

472
00:26:25.173 --> 00:26:29.632
No, then quorum sync (single leader) or consensus-replicated stores.

473
00:26:29.912 --> 00:26:32.835
Must writes continue during a partition from the leader?:

474
00:26:33.115 --> 00:26:36.955
Yes, then leaderless or multi-leader, with conflict resolution.

475
00:26:37.235 --> 00:26:41.011
Are writes geographically distributed with low-latency requirements?:

476
00:26:41.291 --> 00:26:45.899
Multi-leader with key-level homing, or partitioned single leaders per region.

477
00:26:46.179 --> 00:26:50.424
Are there concurrent edits by design (offline devices, collaboration)?:

478
00:26:50.704 --> 00:26:52.816
CRDTs or domain merges.

479
00:26:53.096 --> 00:26:55.464
How much operational maturity do you have?:

480
00:26:55.744 --> 00:26:57.877
Managed single-leader services first.

481
00:26:59.227 --> 00:27:00.123
Part 3.

482
00:27:00.403 --> 00:27:02.131
Architecture and Diagrams.

483
00:27:03.381 --> 00:27:06.923
Component view: orders or payments database replication.

484
00:27:07.203 --> 00:27:08.547
Here is the diagram.

485
00:27:12.397 --> 00:27:16.791
Sequence: happy path, a quorum-sync payment commit and a consistent read.

486
00:27:17.071 --> 00:27:19.866
The sequence diagram walks through it step by step.

487
00:27:20.146 --> 00:27:21.106
Pay to P:

488
00:27:21.386 --> 00:27:27.146
BEGIN, SET LOCAL synchronouscommit = on, INSERT capture, UPDATE order PAID.

489
00:27:27.426 --> 00:27:28.386
Pay to P:

490
00:27:28.666 --> 00:27:29.413
COMMIT.

491
00:27:29.693 --> 00:27:30.823
P to R1:

492
00:27:31.103 --> 00:27:31.786
WAL.

493
00:27:32.066 --> 00:27:33.197
P to R2:

494
00:27:33.477 --> 00:27:34.159
WAL.

495
00:27:34.439 --> 00:27:36.103
R1 to P: flushed.

496
00:27:36.383 --> 00:27:37.322
P to Pay:

497
00:27:37.602 --> 00:27:40.418
COMMIT OK + token = commit LSN.

498
00:27:40.698 --> 00:27:44.346
Ord to R2: read with token (r2 replayed token?).

499
00:27:44.626 --> 00:27:50.429
Ord to R1: fall through to r1 (replayed ≥ token), then PAID.

500
00:27:54.279 --> 00:27:59.591
Sequence: failure path, primary partitioned, self-fences, quorum standby promoted.

501
00:27:59.871 --> 00:28:02.665
The sequence diagram walks through it step by step.

502
00:28:02.945 --> 00:28:07.212
PA to DCS: renew leader lease (times out after retrytimeout).

503
00:28:07.492 --> 00:28:11.161
PA to PA: cannot renew, then demote (stop accepting writes).

504
00:28:11.441 --> 00:28:16.924
W to PA: timer fires at ttl minus safety margin, then node reset.

505
00:28:17.204 --> 00:28:20.852
DCS to S1: lease expired (after ttl).

506
00:28:21.132 --> 00:28:26.017
S1 to S1: am I in the sync quorum and most caught up? yes, then promote.

507
00:28:26.297 --> 00:28:28.687
S1 to DCS: acquire lease.

508
00:28:28.967 --> 00:28:32.188
S1 to App: writer endpoint now points to r1.

509
00:28:32.468 --> 00:28:37.887
App to S1: writes resume (RPO 0: r1 had every acknowledged commit).

510
00:28:41.737 --> 00:28:42.483
Note:

511
00:28:42.763 --> 00:28:45.537
What the system guarantees after this failure path.

512
00:28:45.817 --> 00:28:55.438
Every commit acknowledged to a client was flushed on at least one standby in the quorum, and the promoted node is chosen from that quorum, so RPO = 0.

513
00:28:55.718 --> 00:29:05.531
The old primary stops accepting writes before the new one is promoted: by self-demotion when it can't renew its lease, or by the watchdog if its manager is stalled.

514
00:29:05.811 --> 00:29:13.257
If fencing ever fails, the post-failover reconciliation compares money movements at the payment provider with the database.

515
00:29:14.607 --> 00:29:17.380
State: a database node in a managed cluster.

516
00:29:17.660 --> 00:29:19.004
