00:54:19.737 --> 00:54:24.686
Why failover managers (Patroni etcetera.) pair a leader lease with a WATCHDOG.

827
00:54:24.966 --> 00:54:32.667
Timing model (Patroni defaults): ttl = 30 s, loopwait = 10 s, retrytimeout = 10 s.

828
00:54:35.517 --> 00:54:36.712
Actual output:

829
00:54:38.062 --> 00:54:38.979
On screen:

830
00:54:39.259 --> 00:55:01.254
1,000,000 simulated partitions of the primary. lease only: two writable primaries in 4,033 cases (0.403%), worst overlap 48.3 s. lease + watchdog: two writable primaries in 0 cases. invariant ttl ≥ loopwait + 2 times retrytimeout:

831
00:55:01.534 --> 00:55:04.115
30 ≥ 30, then true.

832
00:55:06.965 --> 00:55:09.248
Self-demotion covers the common case.

833
00:55:09.528 --> 00:55:20.408
It can't run while the manager process or the VM is frozen, and 0.4 % of partitions in this model left two writable primaries for up to 48 s.

834
00:55:20.688 --> 00:55:31.931
A kernel or hardware watchdog armed at every lease renewal resets the node at ttl minus safetymargin, whether or not the process is running, and the overlap disappears.

835
00:55:32.211 --> 00:55:37.267
That's why Patroni's watchdog: mode: required belongs in money-class clusters.

