ADR-038 or 039:

887
00:58:34.903 --> 00:58:40.364
Quorum synchronous replication for money; fenced failover; mandatory reconciliation.

888
00:58:40.644 --> 00:58:41.561
Context.

889
00:58:41.841 --> 00:58:46.961
An async failover lost ~4 s of acknowledged writes (38 paid orders).

890
00:58:47.241 --> 00:58:50.633
Replica reads broke read-your-writes and monotonic reads.

891
00:58:51.983 --> 00:58:52.858
Decision.

892
00:58:53.138 --> 00:59:01.714
(1) Money-class data: single-leader PostgreSQL with Patroni synchronousmode (quorum of 1 over 2 cross-AZ standbys).

893
00:59:01.994 --> 00:59:08.885
Transactions declare durability (QUORUM, QUORUMAPPLIED, LOCAL) via SET LOCAL synchronouscommit.

894
00:59:09.165 --> 00:59:13.389
(2) A commit timeout is an unknown outcome: retry idempotently.

895
00:59:13.669 --> 00:59:14.714
(3) Fencing:

896
00:59:14.994 --> 00:59:24.871
DCS lease, self-demotion, watchdog: mode: required, and writes only through the managed writer endpoint (targetServerType=primary).

897
00:59:25.151 --> 00:59:30.506
(4) pgrewind for every diverged node, with WAL retention sized for it.

898
00:59:30.786 --> 00:59:35.351
(5) Every failover triggers payment reconciliation against the provider.

899
00:59:35.631 --> 00:59:40.879
(6) Replica reads carry a session LSN token advanced on reads and writes.

900
00:59:42.229 --> 00:59:43.253
Consequences.

901
00:59:43.533 --> 00:59:47.608
(+) RPO 0 for single-node or single-AZ failures.

902
00:59:47.888 --> 00:59:50.405
(+) No split brain from process stalls.

903
00:59:50.685 --> 00:59:53.331
(+) Consistent replica reads per session.

904
00:59:53.611 --> 00:59:56.363
(minus ) +1 to 3 ms per money commit.

905
00:59:56.643 --> 01:00:05.219
(minus ) Losing both standbys blocks writes for up to 30 s, then falls back to async (an explicit, monitored RPO exposure).

906
01:00:05.499 --> 01:00:08.336
(minus ) Cross-AZ WAL traffic cost.

907
01:00:08.616 --> 01:00:21.181
Revisit when: multi-region active-active is required (consensus-replicated databases, Ch 27), or managed HA with equivalent guarantees becomes the platform standard.

