1115
01:08:49.754 --> 01:08:54.703
Two cross-AZ standbys roughly triple the database's compute and storage cost.

1116
01:08:54.983 --> 01:08:59.805
Read replicas can double as quorum standbys if their read load leaves headroom.

1117
01:09:00.085 --> 01:09:09.023
Cross-AZ WAL traffic: ~250 GB per day per standby at peak write rates (§4), billed per GB.

1118
01:09:09.303 --> 01:09:11.266
Cross-region DR replication:

1119
01:09:11.546 --> 01:09:14.618
Egress per GB plus a warm standby's compute.

1120
01:09:14.898 --> 01:09:19.207
Sync commit latency: +1 to 3 ms per money commit is cheap.

1121
01:09:19.487 --> 01:09:24.223
The real cost is blocked writes if the quorum is lost, so budget the third node.

1122
01:09:24.503 --> 01:09:25.762
Reconciliation:

1123
01:09:26.042 --> 01:09:31.119
Engineering time, but it's the only thing that catches the residual RPO exposure.

1124
01:09:32.469 --> 01:09:33.408
Part 7.

1125
01:09:33.688 --> 01:09:35.395
Anti-Patterns and Pitfalls.

1126
01:09:36.645 --> 01:09:37.285
1.

1127
01:09:37.565 --> 01:09:39.613
Assuming replicated means durable.

1128
01:09:39.893 --> 01:09:40.703
Symptom:

1129
01:09:40.983 --> 01:09:44.695
A failover loses acknowledged writes (§1.1).

1130
01:09:44.975 --> 01:09:45.701
Fix:

1131
01:09:45.981 --> 01:09:50.418
Know your RPO per data class, and use quorum sync where it must be 0.

1132
01:09:50.698 --> 01:09:51.402
2.

1133
01:09:51.682 --> 01:09:54.711
Synchronous replication to a single named standby.

1134
01:09:56.061 --> 01:10:05.555
Here is the INI code. before: one standby down, then every commit blocks. after: quorum of 1 over two standbys in different AZs.

1135
01:10:08.405 --> 01:10:09.151
3.

1136
01:10:09.431 --> 01:10:11.757
Treating a commit timeout as not committed.

1137
01:10:13.107 --> 01:10:25.331
Here is the Java code. before: timeout, then tell the customer the payment failed, then they pay again. after: unknown outcome, then idempotent retry (same payment id) or status check.

1138
01:10:28.181 --> 01:10:28.885
4.

1139
01:10:29.165 --> 01:10:31.682
Reading your own write from an async replica.

1140
01:10:31.962 --> 01:10:32.773
Symptom:

1141
01:10:33.053 --> 01:10:35.549
I cancelled the order, and it still says placed.

1142
01:10:35.829 --> 01:10:36.554
Fix:

1143
01:10:36.834 --> 01:10:41.613
Session LSN tokens (L2), or read from the primary right after writes.

1144
01:10:41.893 --> 01:10:42.661
5.

1145
01:10:42.941 --> 01:10:46.141
Round-robin reads across replicas with different lag.

1146
01:10:46.421 --> 01:10:47.231
Symptom:

1147
01:10:47.511 --> 01:10:53.549
Status flips back and forth between refreshes (monotonic reads violation, lab Experiment 1).

1148
01:10:53.829 --> 01:10:54.554
Fix:

1149
01:10:54.834 --> 01:10:57.479
Advance the token on reads, not just writes.

1150
01:10:57.759 --> 01:10:58.506
6.

1151
01:10:58.786 --> 01:11:00.365
Failover without fencing.

1152
01:11:00.645 --> 01:11:01.455
Symptom:

1153
01:11:01.735 --> 01:11:08.007
Two primaries accept conflicting writes, and one side's writes vanish on rejoin (lab Experiment 4).

1154
01:11:08.287 --> 01:11:09.013
Fix:

1155
01:11:09.293 --> 01:11:13.538
Lease + self-demotion + watchdog + a single writer endpoint.

1156
01:11:14.888 --> 01:11:15.635
7.

1157
01:11:15.915 --> 01:11:20.245
Pods connecting to database node IPs or caching DNS forever.

1158
01:11:20.525 --> 01:11:21.336
Symptom:

1159
01:11:21.616 --> 01:11:25.029
After failover, some pods keep writing to the old primary.

1160
01:11:25.309 --> 01:11:26.035
Fix:

1161
01:11:26.315 --> 01:11:36.725
Connect through the managed writer endpoint, use targetServerType=primary, bounded connection lifetimes, and a sane DNS TTL (Ch 03).

1162
01:11:37.005 --> 01:11:37.688
8.

1163
01:11:37.968 --> 01:11:39.888
Last-write-wins for user edits.

1164
01:11:40.168 --> 01:11:40.979
Symptom:

1165
01:11:41.259 --> 01:11:45.355
Occasional my change disappeared reports that nobody can reproduce.

1166
01:11:45.635 --> 01:11:46.360
Fix:

1167
01:11:46.640 --> 01:11:51.760
Avoid concurrent writers (home region), or use domain merges or CRDTs.

1168
01:11:52.040 --> 01:11:56.285
LWW only for data where losing an update is acceptable.

1169
01:11:57.635 --> 01:11:58.382
9.

1170
01:11:58.662 --> 01:12:01.265
Forgetting the other standbys after a failover.

1171
01:12:01.545 --> 01:12:02.355
Symptom:

1172
01:12:02.635 --> 01:12:09.526
A standby that followed the old primary won't stream from the new one (timeline forked off before current recovery point).

1173
01:12:09.806 --> 01:12:10.531
Fix:

1174
01:12:10.811 --> 01:12:15.099
Rewind or rebuild every node that diverged, as part of the runbook.

1175
01:12:15.379 --> 01:12:16.083
10.

1176
01:12:16.363 --> 01:12:18.113
Abandoned replication slots.

1177
01:12:18.393 --> 01:12:19.203
Symptom:

1178
01:12:19.483 --> 01:12:25.094
Primary disk fills from retained WAL weeks after a CDC experiment ended.

1179
01:12:25.374 --> 01:12:29.342
Fix: maxslotwalkeepsize, retention alerts, and slot ownership.

1180
01:12:30.692 --> 01:12:31.567
Part 8.

1181
01:12:31.847 --> 01:12:33.191
Interview Q and A.

1182
01:12:34.441 --> 01:12:35.273
Beginner.

1183
01:12:35.553 --> 01:12:36.577
Question 1.

1184
01:12:36.857 --> 01:12:38.819
Why do we replicate databases?

1185
01:12:39.099 --> 01:12:40.465
Thirty-second answer:

1186
01:12:40.745 --> 01:12:55.337
Durability (survive disk, node or AZ loss), availability (promote a copy when the leader fails), read scaling (serve reads from followers), latency (copies near users) and isolation (analytics on a separate copy).

1187
01:12:55.617 --> 01:13:00.545
Each benefit comes with a consistency cost: copies can be stale or disagree.

1188
01:13:01.895 --> 01:13:02.897
Question 2.

1189
01:13:03.177 --> 01:13:05.801
Synchronous versus asynchronous replication?

1190
01:13:06.081 --> 01:13:07.447
Thirty-second answer:

1191
01:13:07.727 --> 01:13:11.119
Async: the leader commits locally and ships changes later.

1192
01:13:11.399 --> 01:13:13.873
It's fast, but a failover can lose the lag.

1193
01:13:14.153 --> 01:13:23.369
Sync: the commit waits until standbys have the change, so there's no loss on failover, but writes are slower and can block if standbys are unavailable.

1194
01:13:23.649 --> 01:13:26.423
Quorum sync (ANY 1 of 2) balances the two.

1195
01:13:27.773 --> 01:13:28.818
Question 3.

1196
01:13:29.098 --> 01:13:32.469
What is replication lag, and how does it show up for users?

1197
01:13:32.749 --> 01:13:34.114
Thirty-second answer:

1198
01:13:34.394 --> 01:13:38.277
The delay between a write on the leader and its visibility on a follower.

1199
01:13:38.557 --> 01:13:47.175
Users see their own changes undone on refresh, see statuses flip back and forth across refreshes, or see effects before causes.

1200
01:13:48.525 --> 01:13:49.592
Question 4.

1201
01:13:49.872 --> 01:13:51.088
What is split brain?

1202
01:13:51.368 --> 01:13:52.733
Thirty-second answer:

1203
01:13:53.013 --> 01:14:02.656
Two nodes both believe they're the leader and both accept writes, usually after a failover while the old leader is still alive (a network partition, a stall).

1204
01:14:02.936 --> 01:14:07.181
The data diverges, and on rejoin one side's writes are discarded.

1205
01:14:08.531 --> 01:14:09.534
Intermediate.

1206
01:14:09.814 --> 01:14:10.923
Question 5.

1207
01:14:11.203 --> 01:14:15.427
How do you guarantee read-your-writes and monotonic reads with read replicas?

1208
01:14:15.707 --> 01:14:17.073
Thirty-second answer:

1209
01:14:17.353 --> 01:14:23.347
Keep a session token equal to the highest log position (LSN) the session has written or read.

1210
01:14:23.627 --> 01:14:28.811
Route a read to a replica only if it has replayed up to the token, otherwise use the leader.

1211
01:14:29.091 --> 01:14:32.889
After each read, advance the token to that replica's position.

1212
01:14:34.239 --> 01:14:35.348
Question 6.

1213
01:14:35.628 --> 01:14:40.044
Explain quorums: why R + W N, and what it doesn't guarantee.

1214
01:14:40.324 --> 01:14:41.689
Thirty-second answer:

1215
01:14:41.969 --> 01:14:53.340
With N replicas, if writes wait for W acks and reads query R replicas with R + W N, every read overlaps at least one replica with the latest acknowledged write.

1216
01:14:53.620 --> 01:15:02.153
It doesn't give linearizability (concurrent reads can disagree), it doesn't resolve concurrent writes, and sloppy quorums can break the overlap.

1217
01:15:03.503 --> 01:15:04.591
Question 7.

1218
01:15:04.871 --> 01:15:09.223
Single-leader versus multi-leader versus leaderless: when do you use each?

1219
01:15:09.503 --> 01:15:10.869
Thirty-second answer:

1220
01:15:11.149 --> 01:15:15.031
Single-leader for most OLTP: simple ordering and no conflicts.

1221
01:15:15.311 --> 01:15:25.914
Multi-leader when writes must continue in multiple regions or devices independently (offline apps, active-active regions), at the cost of conflict resolution.

1222
01:15:26.194 --> 01:15:31.613
Leaderless for high write availability at scale with tunable consistency (Cassandra-style).

1223
01:15:32.963 --> 01:15:33.987
Question 8.

1224
01:15:34.267 --> 01:15:36.251
Why is last-write-wins dangerous?

1225
01:15:36.531 --> 01:15:37.896
Thirty-second answer:

1226
01:15:38.176 --> 01:15:45.856
It silently discards all but one concurrent write, and it picks the winner by timestamp, so clock skew can make an older write win.

1227
01:15:46.136 --> 01:15:48.035
Users lose edits with no error.

1228
01:15:49.385 --> 01:15:50.451
Question 9.

1229
01:15:50.731 --> 01:15:52.161
What is a CRDT?

1230
01:15:52.441 --> 01:15:53.955
Give a practical example.

1231
01:15:54.235 --> 01:15:55.601
Thirty-second answer:

1232
01:15:55.881 --> 01:16:07.486
A conflict-free replicated data type whose merge is commutative, associative and idempotent, so replicas that exchange state converge regardless of order or duplication.

1233
01:16:07.766 --> 01:16:15.105
Examples: counters (G-Counter, PN-Counter), sets (OR-Set), registers, and sequences for collaborative text.

1234
01:16:15.385 --> 01:16:22.723
A practical one is an offline-edited cart as an OR-Set, where a concurrent re-add wins over a remove it didn't observe.

1235
01:16:24.073 --> 01:16:24.841
Senior.

1236
01:16:25.121 --> 01:16:26.167
Question 10.

1237
01:16:26.447 --> 01:16:29.263
A failover lost 4 seconds of committed payments.

1238
01:16:29.543 --> 01:16:31.889
How do you fix it for the future and for now?

1239
01:16:32.169 --> 01:16:33.535
Thirty-second answer:

1240
01:16:33.815 --> 01:16:43.521
Now: reconcile captured payments at the provider against the new primary, re-apply or recreate what's missing, refund where impossible, and communicate.

1241
01:16:43.801 --> 01:16:56.559
Future: quorum synchronous replication for money-class data so the promoted node has every acknowledged commit, maximumlagonfailover, fencing, and an automatic reconciliation on every failover.

1242
01:16:57.909 --> 01:16:59.039
Question 11.

1243
01:16:59.319 --> 01:17:02.925
How does a failover manager like Patroni avoid split brain?

1244
01:17:03.205 --> 01:17:04.570
Thirty-second answer:

1245
01:17:04.850 --> 01:17:09.501
Leadership is a lease in a consensus store (etcd or ZooKeeper or Consul).

1246
01:17:09.781 --> 01:17:18.165
The leader must renew it, a leader that can't renew demotes itself before expiry, and standbys promote only after the lease expires.

1247
01:17:18.445 --> 01:17:21.965
A watchdog resets the node if the manager process stalls.

1248
01:17:22.245 --> 01:17:25.701
Clients reach the leader through an endpoint the manager updates.

1249
01:17:27.051 --> 01:17:28.139
Question 12.

1250
01:17:28.419 --> 01:17:33.069
What happens if a client times out while committing on a synchronous-replication primary?

1251
01:17:33.349 --> 01:17:34.715
Thirty-second answer:

1252
01:17:34.995 --> 01:17:39.005
The transaction may already be committed locally, waiting for standbys.

1253
01:17:39.285 --> 01:17:44.640
PostgreSQL even warns already committed locally, but might not have been replicated.

1254
01:17:44.920 --> 01:17:53.944
The client must treat the outcome as unknown: retry idempotently or check status, never report failure or re-execute non-idempotently.

1255
01:17:55.294 --> 01:17:56.574
Question 13.

1256
01:17:56.854 --> 01:18:00.737
How do you recover nodes after a split brain in PostgreSQL?

1257
01:18:01.017 --> 01:18:02.382
Thirty-second answer:

1258
01:18:02.662 --> 01:18:04.091
Fence the old primary.

1259
01:18:04.371 --> 01:18:10.003
Use pgrewind to roll it back to the fork point and restart it as a standby of the new primary.

1260
01:18:10.283 --> 01:18:15.723
Do the same for any standby that replayed the old primary's WAL after the fork.

1261
01:18:16.003 --> 01:18:20.227
Then reconcile business data, because the old side's writes are discarded.

1262
01:18:21.577 --> 01:18:22.772
Question 14.

1263
01:18:23.052 --> 01:18:28.855
How do Kafka's acks, ISR and min.insync.replicas map to replication concepts?

1264
01:18:29.135 --> 01:18:30.500
Thirty-second answer:

1265
01:18:30.780 --> 01:18:33.148
Each partition has a leader and followers.

1266
01:18:33.428 --> 01:18:47.295
The in-sync replica set (ISR) is the followers caught up within a lag bound. acks=all waits for all ISR members, and min.insync.replicas=2 refuses writes if fewer than 2 are in sync.

1267
01:18:47.575 --> 01:19:00.524
That's quorum-style durability with availability trade-offs. unclean.leader.election.enable=false prevents electing an out-of-sync replica (no data loss, possible unavailability).

1268
01:19:01.874 --> 01:19:02.770
Architect.

1269
01:19:03.050 --> 01:19:04.245
Question 15.

1270
01:19:04.525 --> 01:19:08.215
Design replication for the grocery platform's data classes.

1271
01:19:08.495 --> 01:19:09.861
Thirty-second answer:

1272
01:19:10.141 --> 01:19:17.394
Money and orders: single-leader PostgreSQL, quorum sync across AZs, fenced failover, reconciliation.

1273
01:19:17.674 --> 01:19:22.410
Catalog and profiles: async replicas for reads, with session tokens.

1274
01:19:22.690 --> 01:19:23.842
Carts and sessions:

1275
01:19:24.122 --> 01:19:26.981
Redis async replicas plus DB snapshots.

1276
01:19:27.261 --> 01:19:30.610
Location history: leaderless Cassandra LOCALQUORUM.

1277
01:19:30.890 --> 01:19:31.679
Events:

1278
01:19:31.959 --> 01:19:35.778
Kafka acks=all, min.insync.replicas=2.

1279
01:19:36.058 --> 01:19:40.453
DR: async cross-region, with RPO seconds for region loss.

1280
01:19:41.803 --> 01:19:42.997
Question 16.

1281
01:19:43.277 --> 01:19:46.477
Active-active multi-region for orders: would you do it?

1282
01:19:46.757 --> 01:19:48.123
Thirty-second answer:

1283
01:19:48.403 --> 01:19:57.235
Only if the requirements demand it: region-level availability with near-zero RTO and low-latency writes from multiple regions.

1284
01:19:57.515 --> 01:20:07.179
Simpler options usually suffice: single-writer region with fast failover, or home-region partitioning (each customer's orders written in one region).

1285
01:20:07.459 --> 01:20:15.885
True active-active needs conflict-free design (key homing or CRDTs) or a consensus database with higher write latency.

1286
01:20:17.235 --> 01:20:18.579
Question 17.

1287
01:20:18.859 --> 01:20:23.489
How do you choose failover automation settings (aggressive versus conservative)?

1288
01:20:23.769 --> 01:20:25.134
Thirty-second answer:

1289
01:20:25.414 --> 01:20:28.209
Balance RTO against false-failover risk.

1290
01:20:28.489 --> 01:20:40.521
Short TTLs detect failures faster but promote during GC pauses or network blips, and each failover has costs (connection storms, cold caches, possible RPO exposure).

1291
01:20:40.801 --> 01:20:48.886
Choose TTLs from observed pause and blip distributions, require quorum for decisions, use maximum-lag limits, and rehearse.

1292
01:20:50.236 --> 01:20:51.409
Question 18.

1293
01:20:51.689 --> 01:20:58.153
How would you migrate the orders database to a new major PostgreSQL version with minimal downtime?

1294
01:20:58.433 --> 01:20:59.799
Thirty-second answer:

1295
01:21:00.079 --> 01:21:06.308
Use logical replication (or a CDC tool) from the old cluster to a new one on the new version.

1296
01:21:06.588 --> 01:21:19.025
Let it catch up, verify data (row counts, checksums on samples), switch writers through a brief write freeze, and keep the old cluster as a rollback target for a while (reverse replication if needed).

1297
01:21:20.375 --> 01:21:21.613
Question 19.

1298
01:21:21.893 --> 01:21:29.018
When would you choose a consensus-replicated (distributed SQL) database over PostgreSQL with Patroni?

1299
01:21:29.298 --> 01:21:30.663
Thirty-second answer:

1300
01:21:30.943 --> 01:21:49.567
When you need automatic, per-range consensus replication with no split brain by design, multi-region placement with configurable survival goals, and horizontal write scaling, and you can accept higher per-write latency (consensus round trips), migration effort, and different operational skills.

1301
01:21:50.917 --> 01:21:52.027
Question 20.

1302
01:21:52.307 --> 01:21:55.635
How do you prove your replication and failover design works?

1303
01:21:55.915 --> 01:21:57.280
Thirty-second answer:

1304
01:21:57.560 --> 01:22:06.989
Test it: game days that kill the primary, partition the AZ, stall the process (to exercise the watchdog), fill a slot's disk, and pause replay.

1305
01:22:07.269 --> 01:22:14.992
Measure RTO, RPO (via reconciliation findings), client behaviour (errors, retries, duplicates), and alerting.

1306
01:22:15.272 --> 01:22:18.835
Automate chaos tests in staging with production-like load.

1307
01:22:20.185 --> 01:22:21.081
Part 9.

1308
01:22:21.361 --> 01:22:22.769
Summary and Practice.

1309
01:22:24.019 --> 01:22:25.107
Key takeaways.

1310
01:22:25.387 --> 01:22:29.141
Replication buys durability, availability and read scale.

1311
01:22:29.421 --> 01:22:34.115
Copies disagree, so decide which disagreements you tolerate per data class.

1312
01:22:34.395 --> 01:22:37.424
Async replication loses the lag on failover.

1313
01:22:37.704 --> 01:22:51.421
Quorum sync (ANY 1 of 2 plus standbys across AZs) gives RPO 0 with one failure tolerated. synchronouscommit is per transaction: money at on or remoteapply, low-value writes at local.

1314
01:22:51.701 --> 01:22:55.072
A timed-out or cancelled commit may be committed locally.

1315
01:22:55.352 --> 01:22:58.979
Treat it as an unknown outcome, and use idempotency keys.

1316
01:23:00.329 --> 01:23:07.283
Replica reads need session tokens (max LSN written or read) for read-your-writes and monotonic reads.

1317
01:23:07.563 --> 01:23:12.747
R + W N gives overlap, not linearizability or conflict resolution.

1318
01:23:13.027 --> 01:23:23.651
Split brain is prevented with leases, self-demotion, watchdogs, single writer endpoints and fencing tokens, and recovered with pgrewind on every diverged node.

1319
01:23:23.931 --> 01:23:28.134
LWW silently loses concurrent writes and trusts clocks.

1320
01:23:28.414 --> 01:23:31.635
Prefer key homing, domain merges or CRDTs.

1321
01:23:31.915 --> 01:23:36.289
Watch replication slots, lag, SyncRep waits and timeline changes.

1322
01:23:37.639 --> 01:23:42.951
Reconcile after every failover against an external source of truth (the payment provider).

1323
01:23:44.301 --> 01:23:45.623
